This ascension is dedicated to Terror the starting pet/Large dog.  It
wouldn't
have been possible without him.

I mark this "final" because I have now ascended all classes and posted
all except my priest ascension here.  I still have the notes for it,
if anyone's interested, which  I doubt. It was pretty routine.

Xanthian -- are you still trying to ascend a healer? This is what I
learned from
this ascension and the 5 previous failed attempts:

-- the healer is basically a tourist with more hit points, less gold,
and no
darts.  Thus, it needs to be played the same way and is IMO one of the
more difficult classes ... more difficult, perhaps, than the tourist,
since he starts with no ranged weapons.

Well, let's look at what we have.

I never started a healer with a starting strength higher than 11.
This one started with 9.
Well, that makes him pretty worthless as a melee fighter. The scalpel
is a piece of junk
weapon, also.

Tip #1:  Use the pet as a bodyguard.

Happily, the pet is excellent in melee. So I learned to let him/her do
most of the
fighting. I learned to stay close to him, herd him along narrow
corridors, and dart
behind him quickly when something came along.

Typically, I lose pets in one of two ways in the early game:

A) falling rock trap.
B) mimic in shop.

The best solution to #1 is simply to let him fight 1 out of every 2 or
2 out of
every 3 encounters.  This gives him experience, which gives more hit
points and makes
it possible for him to survive a single falling rock trap.

As towards mimics -- just keep him out of shops until you've swept it
clean yourself.
But it's the pet that spelled the difference between YAAP and YAAD in
this game.

Tip #2: Use daggers.

The Healer can reach "skilled" in dagger, which allows him to throw up
to
2 at a time.  There's your ranged weapon right there. The dagger also
does respectable
damage as a melee weapon. So as soon as I could, I grabbed an uncursed
dagger and used it
against weak monsters in order to reach 'basic' skill.  I also
collected a pile of throwing
daggers which were dangerous at 'basic', lethal at 'skilled', and nigh-
unstoppable when
enchanted to +4.  Up until I found a silver saber, a stack of daggers
was
my ranged and melee weapon all rolled into one.

It's also helpful that there's a nice source of daggers in the Gnomish
mines. Just about
all the Gnomes carry crossbows or bows or daggers, and it's possible
to build up a nice
stack even on the first level of the mines.

Of course, I didn't simply wait until I acquired a dagger. The first
thing I found was
an orcish bow and an arrow trap. After making sure it was uncursed
(another useful feature
of the pet), I used them even though I could never get past unskilled.
It turns out that
any ranged weapon is better than none, especially if the arrows are
poisoned.

Tip #3:  Why do the healer and the tourist start with so much money?
Hmm ... we have some weak guys with no AC to speak of ... and a cruddy
melee weapon ...
but they have a ton of cash.

Could it be ... because they're a walking invitation to the protection
racket?  Hide
behind your pet to get down to minetown, then buy off the priest,
which at Experience level
1 only costs about 400 GP?  It could potentially reduce the AC by 5
after 2 short
donations?

I regret to say I have never successfully pulled off a protection
racket, because
I fight too much, gain experience too quickly which puts the
protection racket out of
reach. But the devteam obviously expected us to do that, even if I
haven't.

Tip #4: The healer starts with 'charm horse'.
The starting comestibles (apples) won't keep the healer on his feet
long, but they do
an excellent job of taming warhorses in the mines. They make good pets
until they start
getting hungry.

Tip #5: Try on *everything* that's uncursed.
Why should one do this? This is why.
a - a pair of jungle boots.
What do you want to identify? a
a - an uncursed +8 pair of water walking boots.
+8!!!???? And it was just lying around in the dungeon! Daaa...ng!

Tip #6: Work on the weaknesses.
The healer is weak.  Fortunately, there are a bunch of boulders
around. When I find one,
I don't push it. Instead, I back up and go around it to see if it can
be used as a 'gym' --
that is , a tunnel or room where I can push it down one way, then turn
around and push
it the other. repeat a bunch of times. Eventually the strength gets
exercised. My strength shot up
from 9 to 14, which allowed me to start carrying a respectable load.

That's all I've got for now. On to the YAAP proper.

